  "source": ["HTB Clicker", "HTB Lightweight", "HTB Jail"],
  "designed_by": "ai-composite",
  "description": "Three Linux standalone machines: Clicker — NFS export → mass assignment (role=Admin) → PHP RCE → SUID binary → PERL5OPT injection → root. Lightweight — LDAP sniffing → capabilities (openssl cap_dac_read_search) → read /etc/shadow → root. Jail — NFS export → custom binary → rvim escape → NFS no_root_squash → SUID bash → root.",
  "introduction": "This scenario teaches Linux privilege escalation via NFS misconfigurations and capability abuse. These techniques appear frequently in OSCP labs.",
  "mental_model": "NFS exports and capabilities are often overlooked. Always run showmount and getcap -r / after initial access.",
  "key_principles": [
    "showmount -e reveals writable shares for source/config",
    "Mass assignment in registration can grant admin role",
    "getcap -r / finds binaries with dangerous capabilities",
    "cap_dac_read_search lets openssl read /etc/shadow",
    "no_root_squash NFS lets you write SUID binaries as root"
  ],

